---
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro";
import CtaBand from "@brandcloud/astro/components/CtaBand.astro";
import Pair from "../../../components/Pair.astro";
import { rules, categories, ruleCounts } from "../../../lib/rules";
import rulesJson from "@brandcloud/antipatterns/rules.json";
import antipatternsPkg from "@brandcloud/antipatterns/package.json";

const bin = Object.keys(antipatternsPkg.bin ?? {})[0] ?? "detect";
const sources = Object.entries(rulesJson.sources as Record<string, { title: string; author: string; published: string; url: string }>);
const reading = ["S01", "S02", "S03"].map((id) => sources.find(([k]) => k === id)).filter(Boolean) as [string, { title: string; author: string; published: string; url: string }][];
const byCategory = Object.entries(categories).map(([key, label]) => ({ key, label, rules: rules.filter((r) => r.category === key) }));
const contents = [
  { id: "why", label: "Why it matters" },
  { id: "five", label: "The five fixes that matter most" },
  { id: "gradients", label: "Gradients: allowed, with one rule" },
  { id: "all", label: "Every tell, by kind" },
  { id: "check", label: "Check your page" },
  { id: "reading", label: "Further reading" },
];
---
<Guide
  title="How to make a page not look AI-made"
  description="The visual tells that make a web page look AI-generated, why visitors notice them, and the fix for each, with side-by-side examples."
  lede={"Generated pages share a look: the same gradients, the same pill above the headline, the same row of icon cards. Here is how to spot those tells on " +
    "your own page and what to do instead."}
  updated="2 October 2026"
  contents={contents}
>
  <h2 id="why">Why it matters</h2>
  <p>A page that looks like a template asks the visitor to trust a business that did not take the time to look like itself. When the same few defaults show up on thousands of sites,
    people learn to recognise them, much as they once recognised a stock theme.</p>
  <p>The rules in this guide come from a review of {sources.length} articles and studies on how AI-generated design looks. There are {ruleCounts.visual} of them, and each has an example
    page in two versions: one with the tell, one fixed, with the same words and layout otherwise.</p>

  <h2 id="five">The five fixes that matter most</h2>
  <p>If you only change five things, change these. Each one is a high-severity rule in the score.</p>
  <h3>1. Swap the purple gradient for one that models light</h3>
  <p>Indigo to violet, or purple to pink, is the colour of generated sites. A button can still have a gradient: one hue, lighter at the top, deeper at the bottom.</p>
  <Pair id="AP-GRAD-01" />
  <h3>2. Remove the pill above the headline</h3>
  <p>A small rounded label saying "New" or "Introducing" over the main heading is a template habit. Put that news in a sentence, or leave it out.</p>
  <Pair id="AP-COMP-01" />
  <h3>3. Break up the wall of icon cards</h3>
  <p>Three or six identical cards, each with an icon in a tinted square, a heading and a line of text, say nothing about your business. Write fewer, longer points, or show the work
    itself.</p>
  <Pair id="AP-LAY-02" />
  <h3>4. Only show proof that is real</h3>
  <p>A strip of logos you never worked with, or big round numbers with no source, is worse than nothing once someone checks. Show a named client, a dated review or a qualification you
    can link to.</p>
  <Pair id="AP-TRUST-01" />
  <h3>5. Clear out template leftovers</h3>
  <p>Old copyright years, placeholder names and builder badges tell visitors nobody read the page before it went live.</p>
  <Pair id="AP-TRUST-05" />

  <h2 id="gradients">Gradients: allowed, with one rule</h2>
  <p>Gradients are not the problem. The problem is gradients with no light logic. A good gradient behaves like light on a real object:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>one hue family, lighter at the top and deeper at the bottom;</li>
    <li>a thin sheen near the top edge and a soft shadow of the same hue underneath;</li>
    <li>used on buttons, offer tiles and at most one coloured section, not everywhere.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>What to avoid: violet to pink or cyan, rainbow and mesh backgrounds, gradient text, blurred glow blobs behind the hero, gradient borders on every card and frosted glass behind text.</p>

  <h2 id="all">Every tell, by kind</h2>
  <p>The full list, grouped the way the score groups them. Each links to its example pair.</p>
  {byCategory.map((c) => (
    <>
      <h3>{c.label}</h3>
      <ul class="g-rules">
        {c.rules.map((r) => <li><a href={`/developers/anti-ai/#${r.id}`}>{r.title}</a>: {r.fix.split(". ")[0].replace(/\.$/, "")}.</li>)}
      </ul>
    </>
  ))}

  <h2 id="check">Check your page</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the <a href="/ai-look-score/">AI-look score</a>, paste your page's HTML and check it at desktop and phone widths.</li>
    <li>For each rule that fires, read the evidence first. A rule is a heuristic, not proof: if you made the choice on purpose, it can stay.</li>
    <li>Compare your section with the fixed version of the example pair, change it, and check again.</li>
    <li>Aim for a score of 0 to 9 with no high-severity rule.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For a live site, or for motion and anything your scripts do, a developer can run the command-line version:</p>
  <pre class="s-code" tabindex="0"><code>{`pnpm exec ${bin} https://your-site.co.uk
pnpm exec ${bin} https://your-site.co.uk --mobile`}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="reading">Further reading</h2>
  <p>Three of the {sources.length} sources behind the rules:</p>
  <ul>
    {reading.map(([, s]) => <li><a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.title}</a>, {s.author}.</li>)}
  </ul>

  <Fragment slot="after">
    <CtaBand
      copy={{
        heading: "Check a page now",
        body: "Paste the HTML of any page and see which tells it has, with the fix for each one.",
        primary: { label: "Open the AI-look score", href: "/ai-look-score/" },
        secondary: { label: "See every example pair", href: "/developers/anti-ai/" },
      }}
    />
  </Fragment>
</Guide>

<style>
  .g-rules { font-size: 0.96875rem; }
  .g-rules li + li { margin-block-start: 0.4em; }
</style>
